1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,793
A clock with only four hours: nought, one, two, three.

2
00:00:04,793 --> 00:00:08,465
Start at two and move on three: you land on one.

3
00:00:08,465 --> 00:00:11,877
Start at three, move on one: back to nought.

4
00:00:11,877 --> 00:00:13,753
Arithmetic that wraps round.

5
00:00:13,967 --> 00:00:23,239
This little system obeys exactly the same rules as ordinary addition, and as rotations, and as multiplying by i.

6
00:00:23,239 --> 00:00:25,492
That shared pattern is called a group.

7
00:00:25,492 --> 00:00:32,822
It is one of three topics dropped from the timetable but still set in NEB papers, so this appendix covers them all.

8
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:43,957
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 17: the appendix of retained topics.

9
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:47,572
Five parts.

10
00:00:47,572 --> 00:00:50,775
Binary operations and the group axioms.

11
00:00:50,775 --> 00:00:53,574
Building and reading Cayley tables.

12
00:00:53,574 --> 00:00:54,941
Rolle's theorem.

13
00:00:54,941 --> 00:00:56,556
The Mean Value Theorem.

14
00:00:56,556 --> 00:01:00,747
And a revision of numerical integration, as you would set it out in the exam.

15
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:11,378
A binary operation combines two elements of a set and must give an answer inside the same set.

16
00:01:11,378 --> 00:01:14,203
Addition on the natural numbers: yes.

17
00:01:14,203 --> 00:01:18,409
Subtraction: no, three minus five escapes.

18
00:01:18,409 --> 00:01:21,664
Multiplication on the integers: yes.

19
00:01:21,664 --> 00:01:25,909
Division: no, one over two is not an integer.

20
00:01:25,909 --> 00:01:27,833
One counter example is enough.

21
00:01:29,033 --> 00:01:33,266
A group is a set with an operation satisfying four axioms.

22
00:01:33,266 --> 00:01:34,217
Closure.

23
00:01:34,217 --> 00:01:35,701
Associativity.

24
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:38,553
An identity element that changes nothing.

25
00:01:38,553 --> 00:01:43,253
And an inverse for every element that brings it back to the identity.

26
00:01:43,253 --> 00:01:47,615
If the order of combining never matters, the group is abelian.

27
00:01:47,615 --> 00:01:49,765
Its order is the number of elements.

28
00:01:50,967 --> 00:01:52,234
Integers and naturals.

29
00:01:52,467 --> 00:01:59,610
For the integers, all four axioms hold, it commutes, and it has infinitely many elements.

30
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:03,715
For the natural numbers there is no negative to undo three.

31
00:02:03,715 --> 00:02:06,179
The inverse axiom fails, so it is not a group.

32
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:09,240
Two short proofs.

33
00:02:09,467 --> 00:02:16,723
If e and e dash are both identities, combine them: the result equals each, so they are equal.

34
00:02:16,933 --> 00:02:25,887
If b and c both undo a, use the identity, associativity, and the identity again: b equals c.

35
00:02:31,467 --> 00:02:36,963
A finite group is best shown in a Cayley table, like a multiplication table.

36
00:02:36,963 --> 00:02:41,985
Here is Z four, addition modulo four: the clock from the start.

37
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:45,066
Read the axioms straight off the table.

38
00:02:45,066 --> 00:02:48,139
Every entry is in the set: closed.

39
00:02:48,139 --> 00:02:52,576
The row for nought simply repeats the header: nought is the identity.

40
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:59,611
Find the identity in each row: one plus three, two plus two, three plus one.

41
00:02:59,611 --> 00:03:03,752
So the inverse of one is three, two is its own inverse.

42
00:03:03,752 --> 00:03:10,015
And the table is a mirror image across its diagonal: the group is abelian, of order four.

43
00:03:11,233 --> 00:03:18,735
The four fourth roots of unity, one, minus one, i and minus i, under multiplication.

44
00:03:18,735 --> 00:03:25,479
Every product stays in the set; one is the identity; i and minus i are inverses.

45
00:03:25,479 --> 00:03:28,461
Another abelian group of order four.

46
00:03:28,461 --> 00:03:34,443
Remember the cold open: multiplying by i is a quarter turn, just like the four hour clock.

47
00:03:35,667 --> 00:03:37,978
Multiplication modulo five.

48
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:44,773
Fill the table: every entry is one of one to four, never zero, because five is prime.

49
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,427
One is the identity; find the ones in each row for the inverses: two and three undo each other, four undoes itself.

50
00:03:53,633 --> 00:03:58,770
With six instead of five, two times three gives nought, which escapes the set.

51
00:04:04,367 --> 00:04:05,606
Rolle's theorem.

52
00:04:05,606 --> 00:04:12,559
If a smooth curve starts and ends at the same height, then somewhere in between its tangent is flat.

53
00:04:12,559 --> 00:04:20,111
Three conditions: continuous on the closed interval, differentiable inside it, and equal values at the ends.

54
00:04:20,111 --> 00:04:24,058
Then f dash of c is zero for some c strictly between.

55
00:04:24,267 --> 00:04:37,342
For x squared minus four x plus three on one to three: both ends are zero, the polynomial is smooth, and f dash of c is zero at c equals two, inside the interval.

56
00:04:37,342 --> 00:04:37,966
Verified.

57
00:04:39,167 --> 00:04:40,096
Two flat points.

58
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,886
Equal end values and a polynomial: the conditions hold.

59
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:52,588
f dash is zero at plus or minus two over root three, both inside: Rolle promises at least one, not only one.

60
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:55,827
When the conditions fail.

61
00:04:56,033 --> 00:05:02,204
The absolute value has a sharp corner at nought: no derivative there, and the slope is never zero.

62
00:05:02,433 --> 00:05:07,039
One over x is not even defined at nought, so it is not continuous.

63
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:15,721
Tilt Rolle's picture and you get the Mean Value Theorem.

64
00:05:15,721 --> 00:05:17,830
Join the ends of the curve with a chord.

65
00:05:17,830 --> 00:05:26,059
Somewhere in between, a tangent runs exactly parallel to that chord: f dash of c equals the chord's slope.

66
00:05:26,059 --> 00:05:32,187
If you drive sixty kilometres in an hour, at some instant your speedometer read exactly sixty.

67
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:38,014
For x squared on one to three, the chord's slope is eight over two, four.

68
00:05:38,014 --> 00:05:40,824
And two c equals four at c equals two.

69
00:05:42,633 --> 00:05:44,028
Exam tip.

70
00:05:44,028 --> 00:05:50,382
Write out the conditions, find c, and say that c lies inside the open interval.

71
00:05:50,382 --> 00:05:52,163
That last sentence is a mark.

72
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:00,746
Finally, numerical integration as you set it out in the exam.

73
00:06:00,746 --> 00:06:06,814
Integral one to two of one over x, with four strips, so h is a quarter.

74
00:06:06,814 --> 00:06:08,845
Make the ordinate table first.

75
00:06:08,845 --> 00:06:13,728
The trapezium rule weights the ends once and the rest twice.

76
00:06:13,728 --> 00:06:17,043
Simpson's rule weights them one, four, two, four, one.

77
00:06:17,267 --> 00:06:24,235
The trapezium rule gives point six nine seven; Simpson gives point six nine three two five.

78
00:06:24,235 --> 00:06:28,740
The true value, log two, is point six nine three one five.

79
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:32,270
Simpson's error is about a fortieth of the trapezium's.

80
00:06:34,700 --> 00:06:35,470
Pause and think.

81
00:06:42,767 --> 00:06:45,282
The identity: it changes nothing.

82
00:06:45,282 --> 00:06:46,555
In Z four it is nought.

83
00:06:48,533 --> 00:06:48,968
One more.

84
00:06:56,600 --> 00:07:01,545
Two: the chord slope is four, and f dash of c equals two c.

85
00:07:03,733 --> 00:07:04,802
Quick recap.

86
00:07:04,802 --> 00:07:08,683
The four group axioms and how to read them from a Cayley table.

87
00:07:08,683 --> 00:07:12,784
Rolle's theorem and the Mean Value Theorem, with their conditions.

88
00:07:12,784 --> 00:07:17,094
And the trapezium and Simpson weights for numerical integration.

89
00:07:17,094 --> 00:07:19,490
That completes Class 12 Mathematics.

90
00:07:23,667 --> 00:07:27,783
That was the appendix, and the end of Class 12 Mathematics.

91
00:07:27,783 --> 00:07:29,984
Well done for working through all of it.

92
00:07:29,984 --> 00:07:31,147
See you in the next subject.

